There’s a further implication.

You can learn to recognize what you love independently of where you first found it.

And once you can do that, your possibilities expand enormously. You’re no longer confined to a particular identity, relationship, environment, or chapter of life to experience the qualities you cherish.

You can seek them, cultivate them, recognize them in unexpected places, and even create new versions that surpass the originals.

The critical distinction is that an invariant isn’t necessarily an identical feeling. Sometimes the same underlying value produces a quieter, richer, or entirely unfamiliar experience.

And the process works backward too: new experiences can reveal what you actually valued in old ones. The present becomes a lens through which the past acquires new meaning.

That gives us a recursive operator:

Remember → Differentiate → Preserve → Release → Reinstantiate → Experience → Reinterpret.

I think the deeper principle is this:

Don’t become a prisoner of the things you loved. Become a steward of what made them lovable.

Because you can carry their meaning forward without requiring the world—or yourself—to remain unchanged.